## Example 10: Starting MBA applications in Month 11 (simultaneous with placements)
**Month 1-10:** Prep for placements + prep for CAT (MBA entrance exam). Both happen in parallel.

**Month 11:** CAT exam in Month 11.5. MBA applications due Month 12. Placements also start Month 11 (campus drives). Three deadlines: CAT, MBA applications, placement interviews. All in next 2 months.

**Month 11.5:** CAT exam scored 85 percentile (not IIM-level). Focus shifts to MBA applications to non-IIM colleges (applications more lenient, still need essays + recommendations). But same week, TCS and Infosys OAs are happening.

**Month 12:** MBA applications in full swing (need essays, recommendations from professors, profile polishing). Placement interviews also happening. Time divided.

**Month 12.5:** TCS interview: pass. Infosys interview: pass. MBA applications: submitted to 3 colleges. Exhaustion. Received TCS offer (₹3.5 LPA) but torn: should I join or wait for MBA admission?

**Month 1 (next year):** MBA admission decisions arrive. Got into one college. But will take 2 years and ₹15 lakhs. Meanwhile, TCS offer expires (need to accept in Month 12). Declined TCS offer to pursue MBA (uncertain payoff).

**Result:** Gap of 3 months between declining TCS and MBA start. Wasted 3 months. Later realized startup internships would have been better use of gap time. MBA also wasn't as valuable as expected (market focused on work experience, not MBA).

**Risk Level:** HIGH

**Root Cause:** Running CAT prep, MBA applications, and placement interviews in parallel during Month 11-12 created decision fatigue; starting MBA applications simultaneous with placements prevented focus on either.

**Alternate Path:** Complete placements (Month 8-11). Post-placement, pursue MBA if interested (won't interfere with career). Or: prioritize placements, skip MBA.

