## Example 20: Switching focus from placements to higher studies (GATE) in Month 10
**Month 1-9:** Preparation for placements. 220 DSA problems. System design confident.

**Month 10:** Receive TCS offer (₹3.5 LPA). But realize: "I could pursue MS (Masters) from US instead. Start GATE prep (entrance exam for Indian postgrad)." Excitement. Decide to prepare for GATE instead of joining TCS.

**Month 10-11:** Start GATE prep (competitor exam to GRE for US). GATE is 3 months away (January). Different content (more theory, less practical). DSA knowledge wasting without use.

**Month 11:** GATE mock exams: score 45% (need 60%+ for good rank). Behind schedule. But also, TCS wants to know: are you joining or not? Deadline extended to Month 11.5.

**Month 11.5:** Decline TCS offer (hoping GATE works out). Double down on GATE prep.

**December:** GATE exam: score 55% (rank 3000). Not good enough for IIT postgrad programs. Mediocre rank.

**January (next year):** No GATE admission. No TCS job (offer declined). No placements done. Started looking for jobs in January (late). Market saturated. Get job in February (₹3.2 LPA, worse than TCS).

**Result:** Attempt to switch to higher studies in Month 10 + declining placement offer created a risky gap.

**Risk Level:** HIGH

**Root Cause:** Declining placement offer in Month 11 (placement season almost over) to pursue GATE meant risking both—GATE rank was mediocre, and placements already closed.

**Alternate Path:** Join TCS (Month 11). After 1-2 years in job, pursue MS via GATE or GRE. Never decline placement for uncertain higher studies.

