## Example 22: Starting to learn React before understanding JavaScript fundamentals (sequence error)
**Month 1-2:** Decide to learn React. Enroll in Udemy React course. Course assumes JavaScript knowledge.

**Month 2:** React course: JSX, props, state. Concepts use ES6 features (arrow functions, destructuring, map/filter). Don't understand them. Backtrack to learn JavaScript basics.

**Month 2.5:** JavaScript course (3 weeks). Learn closure, async/await, promise. Finally understand React concepts.

**Month 3:** Back to React. Now things click. Build first React component (should have been done in Month 1, lost 4 weeks).

**Month 3-4:** React learning accelerates. Build 3 projects. Good portfolio.

**Month 5:** Portfolio complete. But timing: should be doing DSA or system design prep in Month 4-5. Instead, finishing React. Placement interviews in Month 6. DSA weak (didn't practice, was learning React).

**Month 6:** Placement interviews: Facebook (Web Dev heavy): strong React, passed. Amazon (DSA heavy): weak DSA, failed.

**Result:** Learned skills in wrong order; should have learned JavaScript first (foundation), then React (application). Lost 4 weeks to sequencing mistake.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Attempting React before JavaScript fundamentals meant reworking the same concepts; proper sequencing would have saved 4 weeks.

**Alternate Path:** JavaScript fundamentals (4 weeks). Then React (4 weeks). Total: 8 weeks instead of 12.

