## Example 30
**Decision:** "I'll just use LeetCode's 'Similar Questions' feature without practicing diverse problems."

Month 1: You solve "Two Sum" and then do all 8 similar questions. Then "3Sum" and its similar questions. You're solving clusters of nearly identical problems, getting good at variations of few patterns.

Month 2: Tower Research's test includes problems from different domains—graphs, DP, greedy. You've practiced 150 problems but they're clustered in arrays and strings. Your practice isn't diverse. Score 3/10.

Month 3: You realize "Similar Questions" kept you in comfort zones—you got better at problems you already knew, not at new patterns. You force yourself to jump domains—trees, graphs, DP—even when uncomfortable.

Month 4: Qualcomm and Broadcom interviews test breadth. You're catching up but peers who practiced diverse problems from the start have better pattern recognition across domains. Your clustered practice limited adaptability.

Month 5: Off-campus at De Shaw and Rubrik. You can handle variety now but the months of narrow practice show—you're slower at recognizing problems outside your comfort clusters. Competition is with well-rounded problem-solvers.

Month 6: You get Qualcomm at 20 LPA (good but not top tier). You adopt Striver's A2Z sheet for systematic coverage. Similar questions are useful for mastery but breadth-first learning should come first—a lesson learned late.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM  
**Root Cause:** Using "similar questions" feature created narrow, clustered practice that delayed development of broad pattern recognition across diverse problem domains.  
**Alternate Path:** Follow structured lists ensuring domain coverage; solve similar questions only after mastering a pattern; track coverage across all major topics before deep-diving.

## Example 31
